module tecs.audio.fm

--[[
An emulated Yamaha FM chip, played as one voice of the mixer.

An [`Fm`](tecs.audio.fm.Fm) takes one voice slot the way `Audio.play` takes
one, so it counts against `maxVoices`, joins a group, and follows group gain,
group mute, group pause, master gain and master mute like any other voice.
What it adds is the chip's register file: a game drives it with the same
register writes a driver sent to an AdLib, a Sound Blaster, a Mega Drive or an
arcade board, and the emulator renders what the chip would have played.

```nupp
local mixer <const> = tecs.audio.newAudio()
local chip <const> = assert(tecs.audio.fm.new(mixer, {chip = "opl2", group = "music"}))

-- A held sine on channel 0 at 440 Hz, keyed on a tenth of a second ahead.
local at <const> = chip:now() + 0.1
chip:writes({
    0x20, 0x01, 0x23, 0x21, -- multiple 1 on both operators, carrier sustains
    0x40, 0x3F, 0x43, 0x00, -- modulator silent, carrier at full level
    0x63, 0xF0, 0x83, 0x0F, -- carrier: instant attack, no decay, fast release
    0xA0, 0x41, 0xB0, 0x32, -- frequency number 0x241, block 4, key on
}, at)
chip:write(0xB0, 0x12, at + 0.5) -- key off half a second later
```

The interface is registers and ports and nothing else: no notes, no
instruments, no channels allocated on a game's behalf. That is the whole
contract of the real chip, and it is what lets a port of a game that drove one
replay its music driver's output unchanged, timing and all. A note-level layer
belongs above this, in the game or in a driver written for it, and
`tecs.audio.music.fm` plays recorded register logs on it.

## The chips

`chip` names any of the 24 chips the emulator models, by its part number in
lower case: the OPL family `"ym3526"`, `"y8950"`, `"ym3812"`, `"ymf262"`,
`"ymf289b"` and the OPL4 `"ymf278b"`; the OPLL family `"ym2413"`, `"ym2423"`,
`"ymf281"` and the VRC7 `"ds1001"`; the OPM `"ym2151"` and OPP `"ym2164"`; the
OPN family `"ym2203"`, `"ym2608"`, `"ymf288"`, `"ym2610"`, `"ym2610b"`,
`"ym2612"`, `"ym3438"` and `"ymf276"`; the OPZ `"ym2414"`; the OPQ `"ym3806"`
and `"ym3533"`; and the SSG `"ym2149"`, which also stands for the AY-3-8910.
`"opl2"` and `"opl3"` name the YM3812 and the YMF262. `chips` lists them all.

Each chip runs from an input clock, `clock`, which defaults to the one the
chip usually ran at (`defaultClock`) and sets both its pitch and its rate.

`"opl2"` is the YM3812: nine 2-operator channels, four waveforms once bit 5 of
register 0x01 enables them, and rhythm mode through 0xBD. Its registers run
from 0x00 to 0xFF and it is mono, so it plays the same on both sides.

`"opl3"` is the YMF262: eighteen channels in two banks, the second at 0x100 to
0x1FF, eight waveforms, and 4-operator voices from pairs of channels that
register 0x104 joins. Most of that waits on the NEW bit, bit 0 of 0x105: until
a game sets it, the chip behaves as an OPL2 with a second bank it cannot reach,
since the chip itself folds a write to 0x1xx back onto bank 0. Once it is set,
each channel's output is chosen by bits 4 and 5 of its 0xC0 register, left and
right, and a channel with neither set is silent. Bits 6 and 7 select the
chip's third and fourth outputs, which a stereo card did not wire to anything,
and neither do these.

## Registers and ports

`write` and `writes` take a register address the way a driver's own code
wrote one, and send its low byte to the bank's address port and the value to
its data port. A chip with one address port has registers 0x00 to 0xFF; the
OPL3, the YMF289B and the two-bank OPN chips (the YM2608, the YMF288, the
YM2610s and the YM2612s) add a second bank at 0x100; the OPL4 adds its PCM
registers as a third at 0x200. The OPQ has no address port, so its registers,
0x00 to 0x11F, are written directly. `lastRegister` reports the top of the
range.

`writePort` and `writePorts` write the chip's own ports instead, offset and
byte, as the hardware's bus did: offset 0 and 1 are the first bank's address
and data ports, 2 and 3 the second's, 4 and 5 the OPL4's PCM pair, and the
YM2149's data port is at 2. `lastPort` reports the top offset. A register
write and the two port writes it stands for render identically.

## Outputs

Every chip plays as one stereo voice. A mono chip, the OPLs, the OPLLs, the
YM2203 and the YM2149, plays the same on both sides; the OPL3 plays its
outputs A and B; the OPL4 the mix of its FM and wavetable; the OPN2s, the OPM,
the OPZ and the OPQ their left and right. The SSG of the YM2203, the YM2608,
the YM2610 and the YM2149 is mixed in at two thirds of its channels' sum, which
is ymfm's own mix, and filtered of the direct current its square waves carry.

## Memory

The Y8950, the YM2608 and the YM2610s play ADPCM, and the OPL4 a wavetable,
from memory beside the chip. `setMemory` hands the chip an image of it:
`"adpcmA"` (the YM2608's rhythm ROM, the YM2610's sample ROM), `"adpcmB"`
(their ADPCM-B ROM or RAM, and the Y8950's) or `"pcm"` (the OPL4's wavetable
ROM and RAM in one map). An image replaces the whole class from the chip's
next frame, before any write due on that frame, and a reset keeps it. A chip
writing its RAM writes into the image, within its length. Without an image a
chip reads zeroes, which is silence: the YM2608's rhythm ROM in particular is
Yamaha's and ships with nothing here.

## Status, timers and interrupts

Status and timer reads are not offered, and neither are timers, busy time or
the IRQ line. A driver that polled the timers to pace itself paces itself on
`now` instead, and a recorded log is already timed. What that leaves out is
what only a timer can drive: the OPN's CSM key-on and the end-of-sample flags
of the ADPCM channels.

## The clock and `at`

Every writing method takes an optional `at`, in seconds on the chip's own
clock, which `now` reads. The clock is the frames the chip has rendered over
`rate`, so it is the audio clock rather than the frame clock: it does not drift
against what is heard, it never runs backwards, and it stands still while
nothing renders the chip, which is the case before the first output buffer,
while it or its group is paused, and for good on a mixer with no output.

`rate` is the emulator's own rate for the chip at its clock, 49715 frames a
second on an OPL2 or an OPL3 at their usual clocks. The YM2203 and the YM2608
render at three times their FM rate so the SSG can be averaged, and the YMF288
at twice it; for those, and for any chip on a clock fast enough to pass
64 000, consecutive samples are averaged into one frame, so `rate` never
exceeds 64 000. The mixer resamples the chip to the output's rate.

A write lands on the first chip frame at or after `at`, and where it lands
depends only on `at`, so the same schedule renders the same samples on every
run and every machine, whatever the frame rate did. A time within a millionth
of a frame of a whole frame counts as that frame, so `now() + n / rate` lands
on frame `n` from now despite rounding. Writes landing on the same frame apply
together, in the order they were sent, before that frame is generated. So a
key-off and a key-on there count as no change: to strike a note again, space
the writes one frame apart, `1 / rate`.

An omitted `at`, and one already in the past, means the next frame. Sending
each write as it falls due therefore works, but puts it wherever the next
output buffer happens to start, so its timing follows the frame rate. A music
driver sends a little ahead instead: run its tick against `now` with a
lookahead of a frame or two, say 50 milliseconds, and stamp each tick's writes
with the tick's time.

## The queue

Writes cross to the audio thread through a bounded queue the output callback
never waits on, and that thread holds as many again in its schedule for writes
sent ahead of their time. Either is 4096 writes. A write that finds no room is
refused rather than waiting or growing anything: the method returns false and
the write is gone. `writes` is all or nothing, so a tick's writes are never
applied in part, and a batch of more than 4096 can never be taken. A driver
streaming with a short lookahead never comes near the bound. Memory images
have a queue of their own, eight deep.

## Without an output

A mixer with no output device, or one made with a test backend, still makes a
chip whose every call succeeds. On a mixer with no output nothing renders, so
nothing is heard and `now` stays at zero. A test backend that supplies `openFm`
receives every write through the [`FmPort`](tecs.platform.audiobackend.FmPort)
it returns, which is how a harness logs them; one that does not gets the silent
port, which accepts and drops them.

## The emulator

The emulation is ymfm-oxide, a pure-Rust port of ymfm, Aaron Giles's
BSD-3-Clause emulator of the Yamaha FM chips, which the crate holds to ymfm
sample for sample. Tecs links it as a Cargo dependency pinned to one commit,
and `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` carries its licence. The OPLL family plays
ymfm's copyright-free stand-ins for the instrument ROMs. At their usual clocks
an idle chip costs from a fifth of a percent to about one percent of a core;
the YMF288 is the dearest.
]]

local audio = require("tecs.audio")
local audiobackend = require("tecs.platform.audiobackend")
local audiosynth = require("tecs.internal.audiosynth")

--- The writes the queue holds, which is also the largest batch `writes` takes.
local QUEUE_WRITES: integer = 4096

--- The fastest rate a chip's clock runs at: `tecsaudio::fm::MAX_RATE`.
local MAX_RATE: integer = 64000

--- The clocks `new` accepts, in hertz: `tecsaudio::fm::MIN_CLOCK` and
--- `MAX_CLOCK`.
local MIN_CLOCK: integer = 100000

local MAX_CLOCK: integer = 100000000

--- The largest memory image `setMemory` takes: `tecsaudio::fm::MAX_IMAGE`.
local MAX_IMAGE: integer = 16 * 1024 * 1024

--- Names a chip `new` can make: a Yamaha part number in lower case, or
--- `"opl2"` and `"opl3"` for the YM3812 and the YMF262.
export type Chip = audiobackend.FmChip

--- Names a class of external memory: `"adpcmA"`, `"adpcmB"` or `"pcm"`.
export type Memory = audiobackend.FmMemory

--- What one chip is, as `new` needs to know it before the emulator answers.
local record Model
    --- The input clock it usually ran at, in hertz.
    clock: integer

    --- Input clocks per emulator sample: `tecsaudio::fm::Kind::divider`.
    divider: integer

    --- The last register the convenience writes.
    lastRegister: integer

    --- The last port offset.
    lastPort: integer

    --- The memory classes it reads.
    memory: {Memory}
end

local function model(
    clock: integer,
    divider: integer,
    lastRegister: integer,
    lastPort: integer,
    memory: {Memory}?
): Model
    return new Model(
        clock = clock,
        divider = divider,
        lastRegister = lastRegister,
        lastPort = lastPort,
        memory = memory or {}
    )
end

local OPL2_CLOCK: integer = 3579545
local OPL3_CLOCK: integer = 4 * OPL2_CLOCK

local NO_MEMORY: {Memory} = {}
local ADPCM_B: {Memory} = {"adpcmB"} as {Memory}
local ADPCM: {Memory} = {"adpcmA", "adpcmB"} as {Memory}
local PCM: {Memory} = {"pcm"} as {Memory}

-- A chip with one bank decodes registers to 0xFF and ports to 1, one with two
-- to 0x1FF and 3, and the OPL4 to 0x2FF and 5. The OPQ is written by register
-- address directly, and the YM2149's data port is at 2 with 3 decoded too.
local ONE: integer, ONE_PORT: integer = 0xFF, 1
local TWO: integer, TWO_PORT: integer = 0x1FF, 3
const OPL2_MODEL = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 72, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY)
const OPL3_MODEL = model(OPL3_CLOCK, 288, TWO, TWO_PORT, NO_MEMORY)

-- Kept in step with `tecsaudio::fm::Kind` by `tests/audiofmtest.nupp`, which
-- compares every rate here with the one the native chip reports.
local MODELS: {
    [string]: Model
} = {
    opl2 = OPL2_MODEL,
    opl3 = OPL3_MODEL,
    ym3526 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 72, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    y8950 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 72, ONE, ONE_PORT, ADPCM_B),
    ym3812 = OPL2_MODEL,
    ymf262 = OPL3_MODEL,
    ymf289b = model(OPL3_CLOCK, 288, TWO, TWO_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ymf278b = model(33868800, 768, 0x2FF, 5, PCM),
    ym2413 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 72, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ym2423 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 72, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ymf281 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 72, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ds1001 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 72, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ym2151 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 64, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ym2164 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 64, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ym2203 = model(3993600, 24, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ym2608 = model(7987200, 48, TWO, TWO_PORT, ADPCM),
    ymf288 = model(15974400, 144, TWO, TWO_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ym2610 = model(8000000, 144, TWO, TWO_PORT, ADPCM),
    ym2610b = model(8000000, 144, TWO, TWO_PORT, ADPCM),
    ym2612 = model(7670453, 144, TWO, TWO_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ym3438 = model(7670453, 144, TWO, TWO_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ymf276 = model(7670453, 144, TWO, TWO_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ym2414 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 64, ONE, ONE_PORT, NO_MEMORY),
    ym3806 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 64, 0x11F, 0x11F, NO_MEMORY),
    ym3533 = model(OPL2_CLOCK, 64, 0x11F, 0x11F, NO_MEMORY),
    ym2149 = model(1789772, 64, ONE, 3, NO_MEMORY),
}

--- The chip names in the order `chips` lists them.
local NAMES: {
    Chip
} = ({
    "ym3526",
    "y8950",
    "ym3812",
    "ymf262",
    "ymf289b",
    "ymf278b",
    "ym2413",
    "ym2423",
    "ymf281",
    "ds1001",
    "ym2151",
    "ym2164",
    "ym2203",
    "ym2608",
    "ymf288",
    "ym2610",
    "ym2610b",
    "ym2612",
    "ym3438",
    "ymf276",
    "ym2414",
    "ym3806",
    "ym3533",
    "ym2149",
    "opl2",
    "opl3",
}) as {Chip}

--- Configures `new`.
---
--- Read once, when the chip is made. Nothing here is retained.
export type Options = {
    --- Caller-writable. Selects the chip by name, and defaults to `"opl2"`.
    chip: Chip?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the chip's input clock in hertz, from 100 000
    --- to 100 000 000, and defaults to the chip's own, `defaultClock`.
    clock: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Selects the group the chip's voice joins, so the
    --- group's gain, mute and pause apply to it. Defaults to none.
    group: string?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the voice's linear gain before group and master
    --- gain, and defaults to one.
    gain: number?
}

--- One emulated FM chip, playing as one voice of a mixer.
---
--- Every writing method returns whether its writes were queued, which is false
--- only when the bounded queue is full or the chip has been destroyed. None of
--- them raises for either, because a write that cannot be queued is not a
--- reason for a frame to stop. A register, port or value outside the chip's
--- range raises, because it is a mistake in the call. Nothing here is thread
--- safe: call it from the thread that owns the mixer.
export record Fm
    --- Read-only. Reports the rate of the chip's clock in frames a second,
    --- which is the unit of `at`: one frame is `1 / rate` seconds.
    rate: integer

    --- Read-only. Reports which chip this is, by the name `new` was given.
    chip: Chip

    --- Read-only. Reports the chip's input clock in hertz.
    clock: integer

    --- Read-only. Reports the highest register `write` takes.
    lastRegister: integer

    --- Read-only. Reports the highest port offset `writePort` takes.
    lastPort: integer

    @private mixer: audio.Audio
    @private port: audiobackend.FmPort
    @private voice: integer
    @private destroyed: boolean
    @private memory: {Memory}

    write: function(exclusive self: Fm, register: integer, value: integer, at: number?): boolean
    writes: function(exclusive self: Fm, pairs: {integer}, at: number?): boolean
    writePort: function(exclusive self: Fm, offset: integer, data: integer, at: number?): boolean
    writePorts: function(exclusive self: Fm, pairs: {integer}, at: number?): boolean
    setMemory: function(exclusive self: Fm, class: Memory, image: string): boolean
    reset: function(exclusive self: Fm, at: number?): boolean
    now: function(borrows self: Fm): number
    setGain: function(exclusive self: Fm, gain: number): nil
    setStereo: function(exclusive self: Fm, left: number, right: number): nil
    pause: function(exclusive self: Fm): nil
    resume: function(exclusive self: Fm): nil
    paused: function(borrows self: Fm): boolean
    destroy: function(exclusive self: Fm, fadeOut: number?): nil
end

--- Returns what `tecs.audio` lent the synthesizer modules for one mixer.
local function lender(mixer: audio.Audio): audiosynth.Lender
    return assert(audiosynth.lent[mixer], "tecs.audio registered no synthesizer seam for this mixer")
end

--- Writes a register address the way the chip's documentation does.
local function hex(value: integer): string
    if value < 0 then
        return tostring(value)
    end

    return string.format("0x%X", value)
end

--- Returns the model a chip name selects.
--- @raises string when the name selects no chip
local function modelOf(name: string, chip: string): Model
    local found = MODELS[chip]
    if found == nil then
        error(
            "tecs.audio.fm."
            .. name
            .. ": chip must be a Yamaha part number such as \"ym2612\", or \"opl2\" or "
            .. "\"opl3\"; tecs.audio.fm.chips() lists them, got "
            .. tostring(
                chip
            ),
            3
        )
    end

    return found
end

--- Returns the rate of a chip's clock at `clock` hertz, as the emulator gives
--- it: `tecsaudio::fm::Kind::rate`.
local function rateOf(found: Model, clock: integer): integer
    local emulated = clock // found.divider
    if emulated <= MAX_RATE then
        return emulated
    end
    local decimation = (emulated + MAX_RATE - 1) // MAX_RATE

    return emulated // decimation
end

--- Raises unless a register or port and a value are in range for the chip.
--- @raises string when the address is past the chip's last or the value is
---     outside 0 to 255
local function check(name: string, borrows self: Fm, address: integer, value: integer, ports: boolean): nil
    local where = "tecs.audio.fm." .. name .. ": "
    local last = ports and self.lastPort or self.lastRegister
    if address < 0 or address > last then
        local what = ports and " port" or " register"
        error(
            where .. "an " .. self.chip .. what .. " must be from 0x00 to " .. hex(last) .. ", got " .. hex(address),
            3
        )
    end
    if value < 0 or value > 0xFF then
        error(where .. "a value must be from 0 to 255, got " .. tostring(value), 3)
    end
end

--- Lists every name `new` takes as a chip, the 24 part numbers and then the
--- two aliases.
---
--- @return a new list, the caller's to keep
export function chips(): {Chip}
    local names: {Chip} = {}
    for index, name in ipairs(NAMES) do
        names[index] = name
    end

    return names
end

--- Returns the input clock a chip usually ran at.
---
--- @param chip the chip's name
--- @return the clock in hertz
--- @raises string when the name selects no chip
export function defaultClock(chip: Chip): integer
    return modelOf("defaultClock", chip).clock
end

--- Lists the memory classes a chip reads.
---
--- @param chip the chip's name
--- @return a new list, empty for a chip that reads none
--- @raises string when the name selects no chip
export function memoryClasses(chip: Chip): {Memory}
    local classes: {Memory} = {}
    for index, class in ipairs(modelOf("memoryClasses", chip).memory) do
        classes[index] = class
    end

    return classes
end

--- Returns the rate a chip's clock would run at.
---
--- @param chip the chip's name
--- @param clock the input clock in hertz, or nil for the chip's own
--- @return the rate in frames a second, which `Fm.rate` reports
--- @raises string when the name selects no chip
export function rate(chip: Chip, clock: integer?): integer
    local found = modelOf("rate", chip)

    return rateOf(found, clock or found.clock)
end

--- Makes an emulated FM chip and starts its voice.
---
--- The voice starts sounding with the mixer's next flush, which `update` makes
--- once per frame, and writes sent before then wait for it. The chip starts in
--- its power-on state, every register zero, and sounds until `destroy`. A voice
--- `stopAll` or `stopGroup` ended does not come back: make another chip
--- instead.
---
--- @param mixer the mixer the voice plays on
--- @param options the chip, clock, group and gain, or nil for an OPL2 on its
---     own clock in no group at a gain of one
--- @return the chip, or nil when its voice could not start. A mixer with no
---     output is not such a case: it returns a chip that renders nothing.
--- @return the reason, when the first return is nil: every voice is busy, or
---     the backend refused the chip
--- @raises string when `chip` names no chip
--- @raises string when `clock` is outside 100 000 to 100 000 000
export function new(mixer: audio.Audio, options: Options?): (Fm?, string?)
    local chip = (options and options.chip) or "opl2"
    local found = modelOf("new", chip)
    local clock = (options and options.clock) or found.clock
    if clock < MIN_CLOCK or clock > MAX_CLOCK or clock ~= math.floor(clock) then
        error(
            "tecs.audio.fm.new: clock must be a whole number of hertz from 100000 to 100000000, got " .. tostring(
                clock
            ),
            2
        )
    end

    local function made(port: audiobackend.FmPort, voice: integer): Fm
        return new Fm(
            rate = port.rate,
            chip = chip,
            clock = clock,
            lastRegister = found.lastRegister,
            lastPort = found.lastPort,
            mixer = mixer,
            port = port,
            voice = voice,
            destroyed = false,
            memory = found.memory
        )
    end

    local seam = lender(mixer)
    local voice = seam.start((options and options.gain) or 1, options and options.group)
    if voice == 0 then
        if mixer.available then
            return nil, "every voice is busy, so the FM chip has none to play through"
        end

        return made(audiobackend.silentFm(rateOf(found, clock)), 0)
    end

    local open = seam.backend().openFm
    if open == nil then
        return made(audiobackend.silentFm(rateOf(found, clock)), voice)
    end
    -- Opened before the voice's start is flushed, because the start is what
    -- hands the chip to the mixer.
    local port, reason = open(voice, chip, clock)
    if port == nil then
        mixer:stop(voice)

        return nil, reason or "the backend opened no FM chip"
    end

    return made(port, voice)
end

--- Queues one register write.
---
--- @param self the chip to write to
--- @param register from 0x00 to `lastRegister`: 0xFF on a chip with one
---     bank, 0x1FF with two, whose second is 0x100 up, 0x2FF on the OPL4 and
---     0x11F on the OPQ
--- @param value from 0 to 255
--- @param at seconds on the chip clock, where nil or a time already passed
---     means the next frame
--- @return whether the write was queued, false when the queue is full or the
---     chip is destroyed
--- @raises string when the register or the value is outside its range
function Fm.write(exclusive self: Fm, register: integer, value: integer, at: number?): boolean
    check("write", self, register, value, false)
    if self.destroyed then
        return false
    end

    return self.port.write(register, value, at or 0)
end

--- Checks a flat list of address and value pairs, and returns how many pairs
--- it holds.
--- @raises string when the list has an odd length, or a pair is out of range
local function checkPairs(name: string, borrows self: Fm, pairs: {integer}, ports: boolean): integer
    local length = #pairs
    if length % 2 ~= 0 then
        local what = ports and "port" or "register"
        error(
            "tecs.audio.fm." .. name .. ": pairs must alternate " .. what .. " and value, got " .. tostring(
                length
            ) .. " numbers",
            3
        )
    end
    for index = 1, length, 2 do
        check(name, self, pairs[index], pairs[index + 1], ports)
    end

    return length // 2
end

--- Queues a batch of register writes for one time, all or none.
---
--- The batch is what one tick of a music driver writes, and taking it whole is
--- the point: a tick applied in part plays a note with half its instrument.
--- Every pair is checked before anything is queued.
---
--- @param self the chip to write to
--- @param pairs a flat list alternating register and value, as
---     `{0xA0, 0x41, 0xB0, 0x32}`, applied in order. The list is read during
---     the call and not retained. An empty list queues nothing and returns
---     true.
--- @param at seconds on the chip clock for every write in the batch, read as
---     `write` reads it
--- @return whether the whole batch was queued: false when the queue cannot
---     hold all of it, which is always the case past 4096 pairs, or when the
---     chip is destroyed
--- @raises string when the list has an odd length
--- @raises string when a register or a value is outside its range
function Fm.writes(exclusive self: Fm, pairs: {integer}, at: number?): boolean
    local count = checkPairs("writes", self, pairs, false)
    if self.destroyed then
        return false
    end
    if count == 0 then
        return true
    end
    if count > QUEUE_WRITES then
        return false
    end

    return self.port.writes(pairs, count, at or 0)
end

--- Queues one write to one of the chip's ports.
---
--- @param self the chip to write to
--- @param offset from 0 to `lastPort`: the first bank's address and data
---     ports at 0 and 1, the second's at 2 and 3, the OPL4's PCM pair at 4
---     and 5, the YM2149's data port at 2, and the register itself on the OPQ
--- @param data from 0 to 255
--- @param at seconds on the chip clock, read as `write` reads it
--- @return whether the write was queued
--- @raises string when the offset or the data is outside its range
function Fm.writePort(exclusive self: Fm, offset: integer, data: integer, at: number?): boolean
    check("writePort", self, offset, data, true)
    if self.destroyed then
        return false
    end

    return self.port.writePort(offset, data, at or 0)
end

--- Queues a batch of port writes for one time, all or none, as `writes` does
--- register writes.
---
--- @param self the chip to write to
--- @param pairs a flat list alternating port offset and data, applied in
---     order and not retained
--- @param at seconds on the chip clock for every write in the batch
--- @return whether the whole batch was queued
--- @raises string when the list has an odd length
--- @raises string when an offset or a byte is outside its range
function Fm.writePorts(exclusive self: Fm, pairs: {integer}, at: number?): boolean
    local count = checkPairs("writePorts", self, pairs, true)
    if self.destroyed then
        return false
    end
    if count == 0 then
        return true
    end
    if count > QUEUE_WRITES then
        return false
    end

    return self.port.writePorts(pairs, count, at or 0)
end

--- Replaces one of the chip's memory images, from its next frame.
---
--- The image is copied during the call, so the string is the caller's to
--- keep. It replaces the whole class rather than a range of it, lands before
--- any write due on the same frame, and survives a reset. Address zero is the
--- first byte of the string; past its end the chip reads zeroes.
---
--- @param self the chip to load
--- @param class `"adpcmA"`, `"adpcmB"` or `"pcm"`, which `memoryClasses`
---     lists for each chip
--- @param image the bytes, at most 16 MiB
--- @return whether the image was queued: false when eight are already on
---     their way, the write queue is full, or the chip is destroyed
--- @raises string when the chip reads no memory of that class
--- @raises string when the image is larger than 16 MiB
function Fm.setMemory(exclusive self: Fm, class: Memory, image: string): boolean
    local reads = false
    for _, candidate in ipairs(self.memory) do
        if candidate == class then
            reads = true
        end
    end
    if not reads then
        error("tecs.audio.fm.setMemory: the " .. self.chip .. " reads no " .. tostring(class) .. " memory", 2)
    end
    if #image > MAX_IMAGE then
        error("tecs.audio.fm.setMemory: a memory image is at most 16 MiB, got " .. tostring(#image) .. " bytes", 2)
    end
    if self.destroyed then
        return false
    end

    return self.port.setMemory(class, image)
end

--- Returns the chip to its power-on state.
---
--- Every register goes back to zero, so every note stops at once without its
--- release, and the chip's internal clocks start again. Writes sent before
--- this call and due at or after `at` are discarded, scheduled ones included;
--- those due before it still play up to it. Writes sent after it apply as
--- usual, including at `at` itself. Memory images stay.
---
--- @param self the chip to reset
--- @param at seconds on the chip clock, read as `write` reads it
--- @return whether the reset was queued
function Fm.reset(exclusive self: Fm, at: number?): boolean
    if self.destroyed then
        return false
    end

    return self.port.reset(at or 0)
end

--- Returns the chip clock, for computing `at`.
---
--- @param self the chip to read
--- @return seconds of audio the chip has rendered: monotonic, zero before its
---     first frame, still while it or its group is paused, and zero for good
---     on a mixer with no output. It leads what is heard by the output's own
---     latency, and leads the mix by at most a frame or two.
function Fm.now(borrows self: Fm): number
    if self.destroyed then
        return 0
    end

    return self.port.now()
end

--- Sets the chip's gain, before its group's and the master's.
---
--- @param self the chip to scale
--- @param gain linear amplitude rather than decibels, applied from the next
---     output buffer rather than ramped
function Fm.setGain(exclusive self: Fm, gain: number): nil
    self.mixer:setGain(self.voice, gain)
end

--- Places the chip's voice between the speakers, as `Audio.setStereo` does.
---
--- What a pair of chips wired one to each side wants, as the two OPL2s of a
--- Sound Blaster Pro were.
---
--- @param self the chip to place
--- @param left the linear gain of the left side
--- @param right the linear gain of the right side
function Fm.setStereo(exclusive self: Fm, left: number, right: number): nil
    self.mixer:setStereo(self.voice, left, right)
end

--- Pauses the chip's voice, which stops its clock with it.
---
--- Nothing is lost: the chip holds every note where it stood, writes already
--- scheduled wait on the stopped clock, and `resume` carries on exactly where
--- it left off.
---
--- @param self the chip to pause
function Fm.pause(exclusive self: Fm): nil
    self.mixer:pause(self.voice)
end

--- Resumes a paused chip.
---
--- @param self the chip to resume
function Fm.resume(exclusive self: Fm): nil
    self.mixer:resume(self.voice)
end

--- Reports whether the chip's own voice is paused, leaving its group aside.
---
--- @param self the chip to inspect
--- @return true between `pause` and `resume`
function Fm.paused(borrows self: Fm): boolean
    return self.mixer:paused(self.voice)
end

--- Stops the chip's voice and frees the emulator. Safe more than once.
---
--- Without a fade the voice stops at once, so a note still sounding is cut.
--- With one it fades over `fadeOut` seconds, the chip rendering on and taking
--- no more writes, and the emulator goes when the fade ends.
---
--- @param self the chip to destroy
--- @param fadeOut seconds to fade over, or nil to stop at once
function Fm.destroy(exclusive self: Fm, fadeOut: number?): nil
    if self.destroyed then
        return
    end
    self.destroyed = true
    self.mixer:stop(self.voice, fadeOut)
    self.port.close()
end
